window.HU_QUESTIONS=[{"id": "C2-7", "paper": "C2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>A student is asked to prove: <em>for every integer $n$, if $n^3$ is odd then $n$ is odd.<\/em> The student writes:<\/p><p><br> &ldquo;Let $n$ be odd, so $n=2k+1$ for some integer $k$. Then $n^3=8k^3+12k^2+6k+1=2(4k^3+6k^2+3k)+1$, which is odd. This proves the statement.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The argument proves the converse. The statement is true, but this is not a proof of it.", "The argument is valid and proves the statement.", "The argument proves the contrapositive of the statement, so it is valid.", "The argument contains an algebraic error.", "The argument proves the converse, and the statement is false."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The student assumes $n$ odd and deduces $n^3$ odd, which is the converse: if $n$ is odd then $n^3$ is odd. The statement asked for is nevertheless true, and the right proof is by contrapositive: if $n$ is even, $n=2k$, then $n^3=8k^3$ is even. The contrapositive of &ldquo;$n^3$ odd $\\Rightarrow n$ odd&rdquo; is &ldquo;$n$ even $\\Rightarrow n^3$ even&rdquo;, not &ldquo;$n$ odd $\\Rightarrow n^3$ odd&rdquo;.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 4.5}, {"id": "B2-7", "paper": "B2", "n": 7, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ for which $n!$ is divisible by $10^{10}$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$40$", "$45$", "$50$", "$55$", "$60$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$10^{10} = 2^{10}5^{10}$, and factorials have far more factors of $2$ than of $5$, so only the fives matter. The number of factors of $5$ in $n!$ is $\\lfloor n/5 \\rfloor + \\lfloor n/25 \\rfloor + \\cdots$. For $n = 44$ this is $8 + 1 = 9$; for $n = 45$ it is $9 + 1 = 10$. So $n = 45$. Option C is the answer to &ldquo;ten multiples of $5$&rdquo;, forgetting that $25$ contributes twice.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "cnt"], "rating": 4.6}, {"id": "C1-1", "paper": "C1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Real numbers $a>1$ and $b>1$ satisfy $\\log_a b = 2\\log_b a$. Find the value of $\\log_{ab} a$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\sqrt2-1$", "$2-\\sqrt2$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$\\sqrt2+1$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $t=\\log_a b$. Then $\\log_b a=\\frac1t$, so $t=\\frac2t$ and $t^2=2$. Since $a,b>1$, $t>0$ and $t=\\sqrt2$. Now \\[ \\log_{ab}a=\\frac{1}{\\log_a(ab)}=\\frac{1}{1+\\log_a b}=\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt2}=\\sqrt2-1 . \\] Option D, $2-\\sqrt2$, is $\\log_{ab}b=\\frac{t}{1+t}$: the same computation with the roles of $a$ and $b$ swapped.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym"], "rating": 4.7}, {"id": "B1-12", "paper": "B1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $x$ for which the infinite series \\[ 1 + (x-1) + (x-1)^2 + (x-1)^3 + \\cdots \\] converges and has sum equal to $\\dfrac{1}{2-x}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["all real $x$ with $x \\ne 2$", "$x &lt; 2$", "$0 &lt; x &lt; 2$", "$0 \\le x &lt; 2$", "$1 &lt; x &lt; 2$", "$x > 0$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The series is geometric with ratio $x - 1$; it converges exactly when $|x-1| &lt; 1$, i.e.\\ $0 &lt; x &lt; 2$, and there its sum is $\\dfrac{1}{1-(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1}{2-x}$. Outside that interval the formula $\\tfrac1{2-x}$ still makes sense as a number but the series diverges, which is the trap in option A. At $x = 0$ the terms are $\\pm1$ and the series does not converge, so option D is also wrong.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "log"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "C1-4", "paper": "C1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Find the minimum value of $x^2+\\dfrac{16}{x}$ for $x>0$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$6\\sqrt[3]{2}$", "$8$", "$8\\sqrt2$", "$12$", "$16$", "$20$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Split the $\\frac{16}{x}$ so that the product of the terms is constant: $x^2+\\frac8x+\\frac8x\\ge 3\\sqrt[3]{x^2\\cdot\\frac8x\\cdot\\frac8x}=3\\sqrt[3]{64}=12$, with equality when $x^2=\\frac8x$, that is $x=2$. Calculus agrees: the derivative $2x-\\frac{16}{x^2}$ vanishes at $x=2$, where the value is $4+8=12$. Applying AM--GM to the two terms $x^2$ and $\\frac{16}{x}$ directly gives the bound $8\\sqrt{x}$, which is not a constant and so says nothing about the minimum.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 4.8}, {"id": "B1-10", "paper": "B1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The roots of $x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0$ are $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Find the value of \\[ \\frac{1}{\\alpha^5} + \\frac{1}{\\beta^5}. \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-82$", "$-41$", "$-\\dfrac{1}{82}$", "$\\dfrac{1}{82}$", "$41$", "$82$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Since $\\alpha\\beta = -1$, we have $\\tfrac1\\alpha = -\\beta$ and $\\tfrac1\\beta = -\\alpha$, so the required value is $-(\\alpha^5 + \\beta^5)$. Writing $s_n = \\alpha^n + \\beta^n$ and using $s_{n+1} = 2s_n + s_{n-1}$ (from the equation), $s_1 = 2$, $s_2 = 6$, $s_3 = 14$, $s_4 = 34$, $s_5 = 82$. The answer is $-82$. Option F is the sign slip.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie"], "rating": 4.9}, {"id": "C2-10", "paper": "C2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every real $x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x\\le\\sqrt2$<\/li><li>$\\sin x\\cos x\\le\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x\\ge\\tfrac12$<\/li><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x\\ge -1$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "I, II and IV only", "II and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: $\\sin x+\\cos x=\\sqrt2\\sin(x+\\frac\\pi4)\\le\\sqrt2$. II: $\\sin x\\cos x=\\frac12\\sin2x\\le\\frac12$. III: $\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x=(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)^2-2\\sin^2x\\cos^2x=1-\\frac12\\sin^22x\\ge\\frac12$. IV fails at $x=\\frac{5\\pi}4$, where the sum is $-\\sqrt2$. The lower bound matching I is $\\ge-\\sqrt2$, not $-1$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 4.9}, {"id": "C1-5", "paper": "C1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>The set of values of $x$ with $0&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ for which \\[ \\tan x+\\cot x&lt;4 \\] is an interval. Find its length.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>$\\tan x+\\cot x=\\frac{\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x}{\\sin x\\cos x}=\\frac{2}{\\sin 2x}$. For $0&lt;x&lt;\\frac\\pi2$, $\\sin 2x>0$, so the inequality is $\\sin2x>\\frac12$, that is $\\frac\\pi6&lt;2x&lt;\\frac{5\\pi}6$, or $\\frac{\\pi}{12}&lt;x&lt;\\frac{5\\pi}{12}$. The length is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. The identity is the whole question; clearing denominators directly leads to a quadratic in $\\tan x$ that gets to the same place more slowly.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.0}, {"id": "B1-17", "paper": "B1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[ \\int_{-2}^{2} (x+2)\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$8$", "$2\\pi$", "$4\\pi$", "$8\\pi$", "$16$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Split the integrand. The function $x\\sqrt{4-x^2}$ is odd, so its integral over $[-2,2]$ is $0$. What remains is \\[ 2\\int_{-2}^{2}\\sqrt{4-x^2}\\,dx, \\] and $y = \\sqrt{4-x^2}$ is the upper half of the circle of radius $2$, so the integral is the area of that semicircle, $2\\pi$. The answer is $4\\pi$. There is no need for an antiderivative of $\\sqrt{4-x^2}$; recognising a definite integral as an area is the method. Option E takes the whole circle, option A forgets the even part.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 5.1}, {"id": "C2-9", "paper": "C2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, the condition <div class=\"c\"> $\\sin x>\\tfrac12$ <\/div> is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for $\\cos 2x&lt;\\tfrac12$.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$\\cos2x&lt;\\frac12$ is $1-2\\sin^2x&lt;\\frac12$, that is $\\sin^2x>\\frac14$, or $|\\sin x|>\\frac12$. So $\\sin x>\\frac12$ implies it, and is sufficient. It is not necessary: $x=-\\frac\\pi2$ has $\\cos2x=-1&lt;\\frac12$ but $\\sin x=-1$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "trg"], "rating": 5.1}, {"id": "C2-1", "paper": "C2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Consider the statement <div class=\"c\"> &ldquo;The train will be on time unless it snows.&rdquo; <\/div> Here &ldquo;$A$ unless $B$&rdquo; means &ldquo;if not $B$, then $A$&rdquo;. Which one of the following is the negation of the statement?<\/p>", "opts": ["It snows and the train is on time.", "It snows and the train is not on time.", "If it snows, then the train will not be on time.", "If the train is not on time, then it snowed.", "It does not snow and the train is not on time.", "It does not snow or the train is not on time."], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The statement is &ldquo;if it does not snow, the train is on time&rdquo;. An implication is false in exactly one situation: its hypothesis holds and its conclusion fails. So the negation is &ldquo;it does not snow and the train is not on time&rdquo;. Option D is the contrapositive, which is equivalent to the original, not its negation. Option F is &ldquo;not(not snow) or not on time&rdquo; with the first negation dropped, and in any case the negation of an implication is never an &ldquo;or&rdquo;.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.2}, {"id": "B1-6", "paper": "B1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Let $M$ and $m$ be the maximum and minimum values of \\[ (\\sin x + 2\\cos x)(2\\sin x + \\cos x) \\] over all real $x$. Find $Mm$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-\\dfrac94$", "$-\\dfrac52$", "$-1$", "$0$", "$\\dfrac94$", "$\\dfrac{81}{4}$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Expanding, \\[ (\\sin x + 2\\cos x)(2\\sin x + \\cos x) = 2\\sin^2 x + 5\\sin x\\cos x + 2\\cos^2 x = 2 + 5\\sin x\\cos x. \\] Now $\\sin x\\cos x$ ranges over $[-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12]$ (it equals $\\tfrac12\\sin 2x$; or note $(\\sin x \\pm \\cos x)^2 \\ge 0$ gives $|\\sin x\\cos x| \\le \\tfrac12$ with both ends attained). So $M = \\tfrac92$, $m = -\\tfrac12$ and $Mm = -\\tfrac94$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "B2-3", "paper": "B2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>How many positive integers less than $1000$ have the property that their digits, read from left to right, are strictly increasing? (For example $137$ has this property; $370$, $33$ and $71$ do not.)<\/p>", "opts": ["$84$", "$120$", "$129$", "$130$", "$165$", "$512$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>A number with strictly increasing digits cannot contain $0$ (it would have to be the leading digit), and it is determined completely by the set of its digits, which is any non-empty subset of $\\{1, \\dots, 9\\}$ of size at most $3$. So the count is $\\binom91 + \\binom92 + \\binom93 = 9 + 36 + 84 = 129$. Option A counts only the three-digit numbers; option F is all subsets of the nine digits, size unrestricted.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["cnt"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "C1-3", "paper": "C1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>How many integers $x$ satisfy \\[ \\bigl|\\,x^2-5|x|+4\\,\\bigr|&lt;2\\,? \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$5$", "$6$", "$7$", "$8$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Put $t=|x|\\ge0$. The inequality is $-2&lt;t^2-5t+4&lt;2$. The left half is $t^2-5t+6>0$, so $t&lt;2$ or $t>3$; the right half is $t^2-5t+2&lt;0$, so $\\frac{5-\\sqrt{17}}{2}&lt;t&lt;\\frac{5+\\sqrt{17}}{2}$, roughly $0.44&lt;t&lt;4.56$. Among non-negative integers this leaves $t=1$ and $t=4$. The boundary values $t=2$ and $t=3$ give $|t^2-5t+4|=2$ exactly and are excluded by the strict inequality, and $t=0$ gives $4$. So $x\\in\\{\\pm1,\\pm4\\}$: four integers. With $\\le$ instead of $&lt;$ the answer would be $8$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["root"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "C2-2", "paper": "C2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>$P$ and $Q$ are statements, and the statement &ldquo;if $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; is false. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>if not $P$, then $Q$<\/li><li>if $Q$, then not $P$<\/li><li>if $P$, then not $Q$<\/li><li>if not $Q$, then not $P$<\/li><\/ol> Which of these must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "IV only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;If $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; is false only when $P$ is true and $Q$ is false. Then I has a false hypothesis (not $P$), so it is true. II has a false hypothesis ($Q$), so it is true. III has a true hypothesis and a true conclusion (not $Q$), so it is true. IV is the contrapositive of the original, and has the same truth value: false. The single fact to hold on to is that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is equivalent to (not $P$) or $Q$, so its negation is $P$ and (not $Q$).<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.3}, {"id": "B2-2", "paper": "B2", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Let $N = 2^{2026}$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$N - 1$ is divisible by $3$.<\/li><li>$N + 1$ is divisible by $5$.<\/li><li>$N - 1$ is divisible by $7$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Work with remainders of powers of $2$. Modulo $3$ the powers go $2, 1, 2, 1, \\dots$, so $2^{\\text{even}} \\equiv 1$ and I holds. Modulo $5$ the cycle is $2, 4, 3, 1$ of length $4$; $2026 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 4$, so $N \\equiv 4$ and $N + 1 \\equiv 0$: II holds. Modulo $7$ the cycle is $2, 4, 1$ of length $3$; $2026 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$, so $N \\equiv 2$ and $N - 1 \\equiv 1 \\ne 0$: III fails. The trap in III is assuming that because $2^3 \\equiv 1$ and $2026$ is even, something nice happens; the exponent has to be read modulo the cycle length $3$, not $2$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 5.4}, {"id": "B2-16", "paper": "B2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>A student is asked to prove the following claim: <em>if $a$ and $b$ are positive integers and $a^2 + b^2$ is divisible by $3$, then both $a$ and $b$ are divisible by $3$.<\/em> The student writes:<\/p><p><br> &ldquo;Every square leaves remainder $0$ or $1$ on division by $3$. If neither $a$ nor $b$ were divisible by $3$, then $a^2 + b^2$ would leave remainder $1 + 1 = 2$, so it would not be divisible by $3$. Hence at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $3$, and the claim is proved.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The claim is false: $a = 1$, $b = 2$ is a counterexample.", "The claim is true and the argument proves it.", "The claim is true, but the argument proves only that at least one of $a$, $b$ is divisible by $3$; the case in which exactly one of them is divisible by $3$ still has to be ruled out.", "The claim is true, but the argument is invalid because a square can leave remainder $2$ on division by $3$.", "The claim is false: $a = b = 3$ is a counterexample."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The claim is true, but the argument does not reach it. What has been shown is that $a$ and $b$ cannot both be non-multiples of $3$; that leaves open the case where exactly one of them is, and the argument never mentions it. That case is easy to close (the remainders are then $0 + 1 = 1$, again not divisible by $3$), but it has to be closed, and a proof that omits a case proves only the cases it treats. Options A and E are not counterexamples: $1 + 4 = 5$ is not divisible by $3$, and $a = b = 3$ satisfies the conclusion. Option D is false since squares are $0$ or $1$ modulo $3$. The point of the question is the difference between &ldquo;at least one&rdquo; and &ldquo;both&rdquo;, which the student's final sentence quietly glosses over.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["prf"], "rating": 5.5}, {"id": "B1-13", "paper": "B1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>How many chords of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 25$ have length $6$ and pass through the point $(3,1)$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "infinitely many"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>A chord of length $6$ in a circle of radius $5$ sits at distance $\\sqrt{25 - 9} = 4$ from the centre. Any line through $(3,1)$ has distance at most $|OP| = \\sqrt{10} &lt; 4$ from the origin, since the perpendicular distance to a line through $P$ cannot exceed the distance to $P$ itself. So no such chord exists. The instinct to answer $2$ works for chords of length $8$, which need distance $3 &lt; \\sqrt{10}$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "C2-12", "paper": "C2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are true for every $x$ with $0&lt;x&lt;\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin x+\\cos x>1$<\/li><li>$\\sin 2x&lt;2\\sin x$<\/li><li>$\\cos 2x>\\cos x$<\/li><li>$\\tan x>\\sin x$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "I, II and IV only"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2=1+\\sin2x>1$ on this interval, and the sum is positive. II: $\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x&lt;2\\sin x$ since $0&lt;\\cos x&lt;1$ and $\\sin x>0$. III: $\\cos2x-\\cos x=2\\cos^2x-\\cos x-1=(2\\cos x+1)(\\cos x-1)&lt;0$ here, so III is false for every such $x$. IV: $\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}>\\sin x$ since $0&lt;\\cos x&lt;1$. So I, II and IV.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 5.6}, {"id": "B2-12", "paper": "B2", "n": 12, "q": "<p>A sequence is defined by $a_1 = 4$ and $a_{n+1} = a_n^2 - 2$ for $n \\ge 1$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>No term of the sequence is divisible by $3$.<\/li><li>For every $n \\ge 2$, $a_n$ leaves remainder $2$ on division by $12$.<\/li><li>Every term of the sequence ends in the digit $4$.<\/li><li>Some term of the sequence is divisible by $5$.<\/li><\/ol> How many of these statements are true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Each statement is a claim about remainders, and the recursion $a \\mapsto a^2 - 2$ acts on remainders. Modulo $3$: $4 \\equiv 1$, then $1^2 - 2 \\equiv 2$, then $2^2 - 2 = 2$, and the remainder stays $2$ forever; no term is divisible by $3$, so I holds. Modulo $12$: $a_2 = 14 \\equiv 2$, and $2^2 - 2 = 2$, so every later term is $2 \\pmod{12}$; II holds. Modulo $10$: $4 \\mapsto 16 - 2 = 14 \\equiv 4$, so every term ends in $4$; III holds. Modulo $5$: the remainder is $4$ forever, so no term is divisible by $5$; IV fails. Three of the four are true. The first four terms $4, 14, 194, 37634$ confirm all of this, but the remainder argument is what makes it a proof rather than a pattern.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod", "ser"], "rating": 5.7}, {"id": "B1-8", "paper": "B1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Find the sum of all solutions of \\[ \\sin x \\cos x = \\sin x + \\cos x - 1 \\] in the range $0^\\circ \\le x \\le 360^\\circ$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$90^\\circ$", "$180^\\circ$", "$450^\\circ$", "$540^\\circ$", "$720^\\circ$", "$900^\\circ$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Let $s = \\sin x + \\cos x$, so $\\sin x\\cos x = \\tfrac12(s^2-1)$. The equation becomes $s^2 - 1 = 2s - 2$, i.e.\\ $(s-1)^2 = 0$, so $\\sin x + \\cos x = 1$. Squaring, $\\sin x\\cos x = 0$, which gives the candidates $0^\\circ, 90^\\circ, 180^\\circ, 270^\\circ, 360^\\circ$; but squaring is not reversible, and checking in $\\sin x + \\cos x = 1$ kills $180^\\circ$ and $270^\\circ$. The solutions are $0^\\circ, 90^\\circ, 360^\\circ$ with sum $450^\\circ$. Option F is the unchecked sum $900^\\circ$; option A forgets the endpoints.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "prf"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "B2-1", "paper": "B2", "n": 1, "q": "<p>The twenty numbers $1, \\tfrac12, \\tfrac13, \\dots, \\tfrac{1}{20}$ are written on a board. A move consists of choosing two numbers $a$ and $b$ on the board, erasing them, and writing the single number \\[ \\frac{ab}{a+b} \\] in their place. After $19$ moves exactly one number remains. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["The final number is always $\\dfrac{1}{20}$.", "The final number is always $\\dfrac{1}{21}$.", "The final number is always $\\dfrac{1}{210}$.", "The final number is always $20$.", "The final number is always $210$.", "The final number depends on the order in which the moves are made."], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Look for a quantity a move does not change. Since $\\dfrac{1}{ab/(a+b)} = \\dfrac1a + \\dfrac1b$, the sum of the reciprocals of the numbers on the board is invariant. Initially it is $1 + 2 + \\cdots + 20 = 210$, so the final number has reciprocal $210$, whatever order the moves are made in. The answer is $\\tfrac{1}{210}$. Option F is the answer for someone who tries a couple of orders, gets messy fractions, and gives up.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["inv"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "C2-5", "paper": "C2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>A student wants to prove the following statement by contradiction: <div class=\"c\"> for every real $x$, if $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ then $1&lt;x&lt;2$. <\/div> Which of the following is a correct first line for such a proof?<\/p>", "opts": ["Suppose that for every real $x$, $x^2-3x+2\\ge 0$ and ($x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$).", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ and ($x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$).", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ and $x\\le1$ and $x\\ge2$.", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2\\ge0$ and $1&lt;x&lt;2$.", "Suppose there is a real $x$ with $x^2-3x+2\\ge0$ or $1&lt;x&lt;2$.", "Suppose there is a real $x$ such that, if $x^2-3x+2&lt;0$ then ($x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$)."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>A proof by contradiction of &ldquo;for all $x$, if $A(x)$ then $B(x)$&rdquo; starts from its negation: there is an $x$ with $A(x)$ and not $B(x)$. Here $B(x)$ is $1&lt;x&lt;2$, meaning $x>1$ and $x&lt;2$, and its negation is $x\\le1$ or $x\\ge2$. Option C negates the conclusion incorrectly: no $x$ satisfies both $x\\le1$ and $x\\ge2$, so that assumption leads to a contradiction for a trivial reason and proves nothing about the statement.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "prf"], "rating": 5.8}, {"id": "B1-2", "paper": "B1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[ \\int_{-1}^{5} \\bigl|\\,x^3 - 6x^2 + 12x - 9\\,\\bigr| \\,dx. \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-6$", "$6$", "$24$", "$36$", "$42$", "$48$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The cubic is $(x-2)^3 - 1$, so put $u = x - 2$; the limits become $-3$ and $3$ and the integrand is $|u^3 - 1|$, which changes sign at $u = 1$. Then \\[ \\int_{-3}^{1} (1 - u^3)\\,du + \\int_{1}^{3} (u^3 - 1)\\,du = 24 + 18 = 42. \\] Ignoring the modulus gives $\\int_{-3}^{3}(u^3-1)\\,du = -6$, option A, which is negative and therefore impossible for the integral of a non-negative function. A negative answer to a modulus integral is always a signal to look again.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "B2-4", "paper": "B2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be real numbers. Exactly two of the following three statements are true: \\[ a &lt; b, \\qquad b &lt; c, \\qquad a &lt; c . \\] Which of the following must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$a &lt; b$", "$b &lt; c$", "$a &lt; c$", "$b$ is the largest of the three numbers", "$a$ is the smallest of the three numbers"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Which statement is the false one? It cannot be $a &lt; c$: if $a &lt; c$ were false then $c \\le a$, but the other two statements would then be true, giving $a &lt; b &lt; c \\le a$, impossible. So $a &lt; c$ is always among the two true statements, and must be true. Either of the other two can be the false one: $(a,b,c) = (1,3,2)$ makes $b &lt; c$ false, and $(2,1,3)$ makes $a &lt; b$ false. Those two examples also dispose of D and E.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "C1-12", "paper": "C1", "n": 12, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous and satisfies $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all real $x$, and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 f(x)\\,dx=2$. Find \\[ \\int_0^{675} f(3x+1)\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$150$", "$225$", "$450$", "$900$", "$1350$", "it cannot be determined from the information given"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Substitute $u=3x+1$: $dx=\\frac13du$, and the limits become $1$ and $2026$, so the integral is $\\frac13\\int_1^{2026}f(u)\\,du$. The interval $[1,2026]$ has length $2025=3\\times675$, exactly $675$ periods, and the integral of a periodic function over any interval of one period is the same, here $2$. So the integral is $\\frac13\\cdot675\\cdot2=450$. Option E forgets the $\\frac13$ from $dx$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "arint"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "C2-19", "paper": "C2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous on the real numbers and satisfies $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for every $x$. Let $a>0$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_{-a}^{a} f(x)\\,dx=0$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{a} f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^{a} f(a-x)\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_{-a}^{a} x f(x)\\,dx=0$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{2a} f(x-a)\\,dx=0$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I and IV only", "I, II and IV only", "I, II, III and IV", "II and III only"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $f$ is odd. II: the substitution $x\\mapsto a-x$ works for any $f$. IV: with $u=x-a$ the integral is $\\int_{-a}^{a}f(u)\\,du=0$. III fails: $xf(x)$ is even, not odd, and $f(x)=x$ gives $\\int_{-a}^a x^2\\,dx>0$. So I, II and IV.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "sym"], "rating": 5.9}, {"id": "C1-2", "paper": "C1", "n": 2, "q": "<p>When the polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)^2$ the remainder is $2x+1$, and when $p(x)$ is divided by $x+1$ the remainder is $-3$. Let $r(x)$ be the remainder when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x-1)^2(x+1)$. Find $r(3)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$\\dfrac{15}{2}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The remainder has degree at most $2$. Since dividing $r(x)$ by $(x-1)^2$ must leave the same remainder as dividing $p(x)$ by $(x-1)^2$, we can write $r(x)=c(x-1)^2+2x+1$ for some constant $c$. The second condition says $r(-1)=p(-1)=-3$, so $4c-1=-3$ and $c=-\\frac12$. Then \\[ r(3)=-\\tfrac12\\cdot 4+7=5 . \\] The whole question is the choice of form for $r$: writing $r(x)=\\alpha x^2+\\beta x+\\gamma$ and using the derivative condition at $x=1$ also works, but takes three equations instead of one. Option F comes from expanding $-\\frac12(x-1)^2$ with a sign slip.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["poly"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "C2-14", "paper": "C2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>For a positive integer $n$, consider \\[ A:\\ 4\\mid n,\\qquad B:\\ 6\\mid n,\\qquad C:\\ 8\\mid n^2,\\qquad D:\\ 12\\mid n^2 . \\] Which one of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$A$ is equivalent to $C$, and $B$ is equivalent to $D$.", "$A$ is equivalent to $C$, but $B$ is not equivalent to $D$.", "$B$ is equivalent to $D$, but $A$ is not equivalent to $C$.", "Neither $A$ is equivalent to $C$, nor $B$ to $D$.", "$A$ is equivalent to $D$, and $B$ is equivalent to $C$."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$C$: $8\\mid n^2$ forces $n$ even; writing $n=2m$, $8\\mid4m^2$ means $m$ is even, so $4\\mid n$. Conversely $4\\mid n$ gives $16\\mid n^2$. So $A\\Leftrightarrow C$. $D$: $12\\mid n^2$ means $4\\mid n^2$ and $3\\mid n^2$, that is $2\\mid n$ and $3\\mid n$, so $6\\mid n$; conversely $6\\mid n$ gives $36\\mid n^2$. So $B\\Leftrightarrow D$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "mod"], "rating": 6.0}, {"id": "B1-16", "paper": "B1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The curve $y = x^4 - 4x^3 + ax^2$, where $a$ is a real constant, has exactly one stationary point. Find the complete set of possible values of $a$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$a > \\dfrac92$", "$a \\ge \\dfrac92$", "$a &lt; \\dfrac92$", "$0 &lt; a &lt; \\dfrac92$", "$a > 0$", "$a \\ge 0$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>$y' = 4x^3 - 12x^2 + 2ax = 2x(2x^2 - 6x + a)$. There is always the stationary point $x = 0$, so we need $2x^2 - 6x + a = 0$ to have no real roots: $36 - 8a &lt; 0$, i.e.\\ $a > \\tfrac92$. The boundary case $a = \\tfrac92$ gives the double root $x = \\tfrac32$, which is still a second stationary point (a stationary point of inflection), so option B is wrong. The factor $2x$ is the other trap: $x = 0$ is a stationary point for every $a$, so \"no real roots\" applies to the quadratic factor only.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["root"], "rating": 6.1}, {"id": "C2-8", "paper": "C2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, how many of the following statements are true for every real $x$? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$x>2$ if $x^2>4$.<\/li><li>$x>2$ only if $x^2>4$.<\/li><li>$x^2>4$ if and only if $|x|>2$.<\/li><li>$x^3>8$ only if $x>2$.<\/li><li>$x^2>4$ if $x^3>8$.<\/li><li>$x^2>4$ only if $x^3>8$.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;$A$ if $B$&rdquo; means $B\\Rightarrow A$; &ldquo;$A$ only if $B$&rdquo; means $A\\Rightarrow B$. I is $x^2>4\\Rightarrow x>2$: false at $x=-3$. II is $x>2\\Rightarrow x^2>4$: true. III is true. IV is $x^3>8\\Rightarrow x>2$: true, since cubing is increasing. V is $x^3>8\\Rightarrow x^2>4$: true, via $x>2$. VI is $x^2>4\\Rightarrow x^3>8$: false at $x=-3$. Four are true.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "ns"], "rating": 6.1}, {"id": "B1-14", "paper": "B1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>A sequence is defined by $a_1 = 3$ and \\[ a_{n+1} = \\frac{a_n - 1}{a_n + 1} \\qquad (n \\ge 1). \\] Find the value of the product $a_1 a_2 a_3 \\cdots a_{2026}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-2$", "$-\\dfrac12$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$3$", "$\\dfrac94$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Compute a few terms: $a_1 = 3$, $a_2 = \\tfrac12$, $a_3 = -\\tfrac13$, $a_4 = -2$, $a_5 = 3$. The sequence has period $4$. (Structurally, if $g(x) = \\tfrac{x-1}{x+1}$ then $g(g(x)) = -\\tfrac1x$, so $g$ has order $4$.) The product over one period is $3\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot(-\\tfrac13)\\cdot(-2) = 1$. Since $2026 = 4\\cdot 506 + 2$, the product is $a_1 a_2 = \\tfrac32$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "inv"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "C1-16", "paper": "C1", "n": 16, "q": "<p>The continuous function $f$ satisfies \\[ f(x)=x^2+x\\int_0^1 t\\,f(t)\\,dt\\qquad\\text{for all } x . \\] Find $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{11}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{48}$", "$\\dfrac{13}{24}$", "$\\dfrac{7}{12}$", "$\\dfrac58$", "$\\dfrac23$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The integral $\\int_0^1 tf(t)\\,dt$ is just a number; call it $B$. Then $f(x)=x^2+Bx$, and substituting back, \\[ B=\\int_0^1 t(t^2+Bt)\\,dt=\\tfrac14+\\tfrac B3, \\] so $B=\\frac38$. Hence $\\int_0^1 f=\\frac13+\\frac B2=\\frac13+\\frac3{16}=\\frac{25}{48}$. Option A comes from stopping at $B=\\frac14$, having dropped the self-referential term.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "C2-4", "paper": "C2", "n": 4, "q": "<p>A function $f$ from the real numbers to the real numbers is called <em>increasing<\/em> if, for all real $x$ and $y$, if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)&lt;f(y)$. Which one of the following is equivalent to &ldquo;$f$ is not increasing&rdquo;?<\/p>", "opts": ["For all real $x$ and $y$, if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ such that, if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ge y$ and $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "For all real $x$ and $y$, $x\\ge y$ or $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ with $x&lt;y$ or $f(x)\\ge f(y)$.", "There exist real $x$ and $y$ with $x&lt;y$ and $f(x)\\ge f(y)$."], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>&ldquo;Increasing&rdquo; is: for all $x,y$, (if $x&lt;y$ then $f(x)&lt;f(y)$). Negating swaps the quantifier and negates the implication, and the negation of &ldquo;if $A$ then $B$&rdquo; is &ldquo;$A$ and not $B$&rdquo;: there exist $x,y$ with $x&lt;y$ and $f(x)\\ge f(y)$. Option B is the classic mistake of negating only the conclusion. It is true for every function whatsoever, since choosing $x=y$ makes the hypothesis $x&lt;y$ false and the implication vacuously true.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 6.2}, {"id": "B2-5", "paper": "B2", "n": 5, "q": "<p>For which positive integers $n$ is it possible to fill an $n \\times n$ grid with integers so that the sum of the entries in every row is odd and the sum of the entries in every column is even?<\/p>", "opts": ["Every $n \\ge 1$", "Even $n$ only", "Odd $n$ only", "No $n$", "Every $n \\ge 2$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Add up every entry in the grid in two ways. Summing the rows gives a sum of $n$ odd numbers, which is odd exactly when $n$ is odd; summing the columns gives a sum of $n$ even numbers, which is always even. So $n$ odd is impossible. For $n$ even, put $1$ in every cell of the first column and $0$ everywhere else: every row sums to $1$ and the columns sum to $n$ (even) and $0$. The answer is even $n$ only. Counting one quantity two ways is the whole method.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["col", "two"], "rating": 6.3}, {"id": "C1-19", "paper": "C1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The tangent to the curve $y=x^3$ at the point $P(1,1)$ meets the curve again at $Q$. Find the area of the region enclosed between the curve and the line segment $PQ$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac94$", "$\\dfrac{27}{8}$", "$\\dfrac{27}{4}$", "$8$", "$\\dfrac{27}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{81}{4}$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The tangent at $P$ is $y=3x-2$, and $x^3-(3x-2)=x^3-3x+2=(x-1)^2(x+2)$, where the double factor is forced by tangency. So $Q$ has $x=-2$, and the curve lies above the tangent on $(-2,1)$. With $u=x-1$, \\[ \\int_{-2}^{1}(x-1)^2(x+2)\\,dx=\\int_{-3}^{0}(u^3+3u^2)\\,du=0-\\left(\\tfrac{81}4-27\\right)=\\tfrac{27}{4} . \\] Knowing that the factorisation must contain $(x-1)^2$ saves the polynomial division, and the shift $u=x-1$ saves expanding.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "poly"], "rating": 6.3}, {"id": "B1-19", "paper": "B1", "n": 19, "q": "<p>A function $f$ satisfies \\[ \\int_0^2 f(2x)\\,dx = 3, \\qquad \\int_1^3 f(x+1)\\,dx = 5, \\qquad \\int_0^1 f(3-3x)\\,dx = 2. \\] Find the value of $\\displaystyle\\int_3^4 f(x)\\,dx$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-1$", "$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$5$", "$6$", "cannot be determined from the information given"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Substitute in each integral. In the first, $u = 2x$ gives $\\tfrac12\\int_0^4 f = 3$, so $\\int_0^4 f = 6$. In the second, $u = x+1$ gives $\\int_2^4 f = 5$. In the third, $u = 3 - 3x$ reverses the limits and contributes a factor $\\tfrac13$, giving $\\tfrac13\\int_0^3 f = 2$, so $\\int_0^3 f = 6$. Then \\[ \\int_3^4 f = \\int_0^4 f - \\int_0^3 f = 6 - 6 = 0. \\] The information is enough, so option G is wrong; the value $5$ in option E is $\\int_2^4 f$, read off carelessly.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "C1-10", "paper": "C1", "n": 10, "q": "<p>The two tangents from the origin to the circle $(x-4)^2+(y-3)^2=4$ meet at an acute angle $\\theta$. Find $\\tan\\theta$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac25$", "$\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}$", "$\\dfrac{12}{17}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\sqrt{21}}{17}$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>The centre $C=(4,3)$ is at distance $5$ from the origin $O$ and the radius is $2$. Each tangent makes an angle $\\alpha$ with $OC$ where $\\sin\\alpha=\\frac25$, so $\\tan\\alpha=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}$, and the angle between the tangents is $\\theta=2\\alpha$, which is acute since $\\alpha&lt;\\frac\\pi4$. Then \\[ \\tan\\theta=\\frac{2\\tan\\alpha}{1-\\tan^2\\alpha}=\\frac{4/\\sqrt{21}}{17/21}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{17} . \\] The coordinate route, requiring $y=mx$ to be at distance $2$ from $C$, gives $12m^2-24m+5=0$ and reaches the same value through $\\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}$, with more work. Option B is $\\tan\\alpha$, the half-angle.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "trg"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "C2-11", "paper": "C2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>A student solves the inequality $\\sin x\\cos x>\\cos^2x$ for $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$ as follows.<\/p><p><br> &ldquo;When $\\cos x=0$, both sides are $0$, so the inequality fails. Otherwise $\\cos^2x>0$, so I can divide by it without changing the direction of the inequality, giving $\\tan x>1$. Hence the solution set is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$ together with $\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.&rdquo;<\/p><p><br> Which of the following is correct?<\/p>", "opts": ["The final answer is correct and the argument is valid.", "The final answer is wrong, because dividing by $\\cos^2x$ may reverse the inequality.", "The final answer is wrong, because solutions with $\\cos x&lt;0$ have been lost.", "The final answer is wrong, because it includes values where $\\cos x=0$.", "The final answer is correct, but the argument is invalid.", "The final answer is wrong; the solution set is $\\tfrac{\\pi}{4}&lt;x&lt;\\tfrac{5\\pi}{4}$."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The student handles $\\cos x=0$ separately, where the inequality reads $0>0$ and fails, and elsewhere divides by $\\cos^2x>0$, which is legitimate and preserves the direction. So the original inequality is equivalent to $\\tan x>1$ with $\\cos x\\ne0$, whose solution set in $[0,2\\pi)$ is exactly the one given. Compare this with dividing $\\sin x>\\cos x$ by $\\cos x$, where the sign of $\\cos x$ matters: the difference is that $\\cos^2x$ is never negative.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["prf", "trg"], "rating": 6.4}, {"id": "B1-20", "paper": "B1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Let $f(x) = x^3 + x$, and let $f^{-1}$ denote the inverse function of $f$. Find the value of \\[ \\int_0^{10} f^{-1}(x)\\,dx. \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$10$", "$14$", "$16$", "$20$", "$34$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Do not try to invert $f$. Since $f$ is increasing and $f(2) = 10$, the graph of $f^{-1}$ on $[0,10]$ is the reflection of the graph of $f$ on $[0,2]$ in the line $y = x$, and together the two regions fill the $2\\times 10$ rectangle: \\[ \\int_0^{10} f^{-1}(x)\\,dx = 2\\cdot 10 - \\int_0^2 (x^3 + x)\\,dx = 20 - (4 + 2) = 14. \\] Option A is $\\int_0^2 f$, the wrong piece of the rectangle.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "B2-14", "paper": "B2", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Let $A$, $B$, $C$ be the angles of a triangle. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\sin A + \\sin B > \\sin C$<\/li><li>$\\cos A + \\cos B > 0$<\/li><li>$\\sin A \\sin B > \\cos C$<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true for every triangle?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: by the sine rule the sides are proportional to the sines of the opposite angles, so $\\sin A + \\sin B > \\sin C$ is the triangle inequality $a + b > c$ in disguise. True. II: since $A + B &lt; \\pi$, we have $B &lt; \\pi - A$, and cosine is decreasing on $(0,\\pi)$, so $\\cos B > \\cos(\\pi - A) = -\\cos A$. True. III: $\\cos C = -\\cos(A + B) = \\sin A \\sin B - \\cos A \\cos B$, so $\\sin A \\sin B > \\cos C$ is equivalent to $\\cos A \\cos B > 0$, which fails whenever one of $A$, $B$ is obtuse. For instance $A = 120^\\circ$, $B = C = 30^\\circ$ gives $\\sin A \\sin B = \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{4} &lt; \\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2} = \\cos C$. False. The answer is I and II. Each statement is a triangle fact wearing trigonometric clothing: the triangle inequality, the angle sum, and the sign of $\\cos A \\cos B$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "B2-20", "paper": "B2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>A pile contains $2026$ stones. Two players move alternately; a move consists of removing either $2$ or $3$ stones from the pile. A player who cannot move loses. With best play by both sides, which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["The second player wins.", "The first player wins, and removing $2$ stones is their only winning first move.", "The first player wins, and removing $3$ stones is their only winning first move.", "The first player wins, and removing either $2$ or $3$ stones is a winning first move.", "The result depends on choices made later in the game."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Classify pile sizes as losing (L, the player to move loses) or winning (W). $0$ and $1$ are L, since no move is possible. Then $2 \\to 0$ and $3 \\to 1$ are W; $4 \\to 1$ is W; $5$ can only reach $3$ and $2$, both W, so $5$ is L; $6$ reaches $4$ and $3$, both W, so L; $7 \\to 5$ is W; $8 \\to 6$ is W; $9 \\to 6$ is W; $10$ reaches $8$ and $7$, both W, so L; $11$ reaches $9$ and $8$, so L. The pattern is periodic with period $5$: the losing sizes are those leaving remainder $0$ or $1$ on division by $5$. Since $2026 = 5 \\cdot 405 + 1$, the starting position is losing for the player to move: the second player wins. Options B to D assume the first player has a good move; option E misunderstands what best play means.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["game"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "C1-14", "paper": "C1", "n": 14, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=x^3+3x$, and let $f^{-1}$ be the inverse function of $f$. Find \\[ \\int_4^{14} f^{-1}(x)\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{33}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{63}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{79}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{95}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{111}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{129}{4}$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing with $f(1)=4$ and $f(2)=14$. On $[4,14]$, the graph of $f^{-1}$ is the reflection of the graph of $f$ on $[1,2]$, and the two areas fill the $L$-shaped region between the rectangles $[0,2]\\times[0,14]$ and $[0,1]\\times[0,4]$: \\[ \\int_4^{14}f^{-1}(x)\\,dx=2\\cdot14-1\\cdot4-\\int_1^2(x^3+3x)\\,dx=24-\\left(\\tfrac{15}{4}+\\tfrac92\\right)=24-\\tfrac{33}{4}=\\tfrac{63}{4} . \\] Option C forgets to subtract the small rectangle $1\\cdot4$: the formula is $bf(b)-af(a)-\\int_a^bf$, and the $af(a)$ term is only zero when $a=0$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "C2-6", "paper": "C2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Consider the statement <div class=\"c\"> for all real $x$, if $\\sin x>0$ or $\\cos x>0$, then $\\sin x+\\cos x>0$. <\/div> How many of the following values of $x$ are counterexamples to this statement? \\[ \\frac{\\pi}{6},\\qquad \\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad \\frac{5\\pi}{6},\\qquad \\frac{7\\pi}{6},\\qquad \\frac{5\\pi}{3},\\qquad \\frac{7\\pi}{4} \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>A counterexample needs the hypothesis true and the conclusion false. At $\\frac\\pi6$ and $\\frac{2\\pi}3$ the hypothesis holds and $\\sin x+\\cos x>0$. At $\\frac{5\\pi}6$ the hypothesis holds ($\\sin x=\\frac12$) and $\\sin x+\\cos x=\\frac{1-\\sqrt3}{2}&lt;0$: a counterexample. At $\\frac{7\\pi}6$ both are negative, so the hypothesis fails and this is not a counterexample, however negative the sum. At $\\frac{5\\pi}3$, $\\cos x=\\frac12>0$ and the sum is $\\frac{1-\\sqrt3}{2}&lt;0$: a counterexample. At $\\frac{7\\pi}4$, $\\cos x>0$ and the sum is exactly $0$, which is not $>0$: a counterexample. Three in total.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "trg"], "rating": 6.5}, {"id": "B2-9", "paper": "B2", "n": 9, "q": "<p>How many ordered triples $(x, y, z)$ of integers satisfy $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 2023$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$8$", "$24$", "$48$", "$96$", "Infinitely many"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>A square leaves remainder $0$, $1$ or $4$ on division by $8$. Adding three such remainders can give $0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6$ but never $7$ (the largest available combinations are $4+4+4 \\equiv 4$, $4+4+1 \\equiv 1$, $4+1+1 = 6$, $1+1+1 = 3$). Since $2023 = 8 \\cdot 252 + 7$, there are no solutions at all. No searching is needed, which is the point: the options B to E are the counts one gets after finding one solution and multiplying by sign and permutation symmetries, and there is no solution to start from.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "C1-6", "paper": "C1", "n": 6, "q": "<p>For a real number $k$, say that the equation \\[ \\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(2\\sin x)^n=k \\] is <em>solvable<\/em> if there is a real $x$ for which the series converges and its sum is $k$. For how many of the values $k=\\tfrac13,\\ \\tfrac12,\\ \\tfrac23,\\ 2,\\ 4$ is the equation solvable?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The series is geometric with ratio $2\\sin x$, so it converges only when $|2\\sin x|&lt;1$, and then its sum is $\\frac{1}{1-2\\sin x}$. Setting this equal to $k$ gives $2\\sin x=1-\\frac1k$, and convergence requires $\\left|1-\\frac1k\\right|&lt;1$, that is $0&lt;\\frac1k&lt;2$, or $k>\\frac12$. Any such $k$ gives $|\\sin x|&lt;\\frac12$, so a real $x$ exists. Hence $k=\\frac23,2,4$ work and $k=\\frac13,\\frac12$ do not: three values. The trap is $k=\\frac13$, where solving the formula gives $\\sin x=-1$, a perfectly good value of sine at which the series diverges.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ser", "trg"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "C2-17", "paper": "C2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>For real numbers $a$ and $b$, consider the four conditions \\[ (1)\\ a+b>0,\\qquad (2)\\ ab>0,\\qquad (3)\\ a>0,\\qquad (4)\\ b>0 . \\] How many of the six pairs of these conditions are such that the two conditions together are equivalent to all four conditions holding?<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>All four together are equivalent to $a>0$ and $b>0$. Test the six pairs: (1)(2): $ab>0$ means same sign, and a positive sum makes both positive; equivalent. (3)(4): trivially equivalent. (2)(3): $a>0$ and $ab>0$ give $b>0$; equivalent. (2)(4): likewise. (1)(3): $a=5$, $b=-1$ satisfies both but not (4). (1)(4): symmetric. So four pairs. Every single condition is also individually removable here, which shows that &ldquo;removable one at a time&rdquo; and &ldquo;which small subsets suffice&rdquo; are different questions.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "ineq"], "rating": 6.6}, {"id": "B2-11", "paper": "B2", "n": 11, "q": "<p>For a convex quadrilateral, the condition <div class=\"c\"> &ldquo;the midpoints of the four sides are the vertices of a square&rdquo; <\/div> is <span class=\"blank\">________<\/span> for the quadrilateral to be a square.<\/p>", "opts": ["necessary but not sufficient", "sufficient but not necessary", "necessary and sufficient", "neither necessary nor sufficient"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>The midpoints of the sides of any quadrilateral form a parallelogram whose sides are parallel to the diagonals and half their length. That parallelogram is a square exactly when the diagonals are equal in length and perpendicular. A square has such diagonals, so the condition is necessary. It is not sufficient: the quadrilateral with vertices $(-2,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$, $(0,-3)$ is convex, has perpendicular diagonals both of length $4$, and is a kite, not a square. The condition is necessary but not sufficient.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "circ"], "rating": 6.7}, {"id": "C2-3", "paper": "C2", "n": 3, "q": "<p>Which of the following statements are logically equivalent to &ldquo;if $P$, then $Q$ or $R$&rdquo;? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>If $P$ and not $Q$, then $R$.<\/li><li>If not $Q$ and not $R$, then not $P$.<\/li><li>Either &ldquo;if $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; is true, or &ldquo;if $P$ then $R$&rdquo; is true (or both).<\/li><li>&ldquo;If $P$ then $Q$&rdquo; and &ldquo;if $P$ then $R$&rdquo; are both true.<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "I and II only", "I, II and III only", "II and III only", "I and III only", "I, II, III and IV"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Write each as a disjunction. The original is (not $P$) or $Q$ or $R$. I is not$(P$ and not $Q)$ or $R$, which is (not $P$) or $Q$ or $R$: equivalent. II is the contrapositive of the original: equivalent. III is ((not $P$) or $Q$) or ((not $P$) or $R$), which simplifies to (not $P$) or $Q$ or $R$: equivalent. IV is ((not $P$) or $Q$) and ((not $P$) or $R$), which is (not $P$) or ($Q$ and $R$): stronger, and false when $P$ and $Q$ are true and $R$ is false.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 6.7}, {"id": "B1-11", "paper": "B1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>There are exactly two straight lines that are tangent to both of the curves \\[ y = x^2 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y = -x^2 + 6x - 10. \\] Find the product of the gradients of these two lines.<\/p>", "opts": ["$-4$", "$-2$", "$-1$", "$1$", "$2$", "$4$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>The tangent to $y = x^2$ at $x = a$ is $y = 2ax - a^2$; the tangent to the second curve at $x = b$ is $y = (6-2b)x + b^2 - 10$. Matching gradients and intercepts, $a = 3 - b$ and $-(3-b)^2 = b^2 - 10$, which reduces to $2b^2 - 6b - 1 = 0$. The two common tangents correspond to the two roots $b_1, b_2$, with gradients $6 - 2b_i$, so \\[ (6-2b_1)(6-2b_2) = 36 - 12(b_1+b_2) + 4b_1b_2 = 36 - 36 - 2 = -2 \\] by Vieta, without solving for $b$ at all. (The gradients are $3 \\pm \\sqrt{11}$.)<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie", "poly"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "C1-9", "paper": "C1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $a$ for which the equation \\[ x^3-3ax^2+4=0 \\] has exactly one real solution.<\/p>", "opts": ["$a&lt;1$", "$a&lt;1$ and $a\\ne0$", "$0&lt;a&lt;1$", "$a>1$", "$a\\ne1$", "$a&lt;-1$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=x^3-3ax^2+4$, so $g'(x)=3x(x-2a)$. If $a=0$, $g$ is increasing and has exactly one root. Otherwise the stationary values are $g(0)=4$ and $g(2a)=4-4a^3$, and there is exactly one real root precisely when these have the same sign, that is $4-4a^3>0$, or $a&lt;1$. At $a=1$, $g(x)=(x-2)^2(x+1)$ has two distinct roots, so $a=1$ is excluded. Hence $a&lt;1$, including $a=0$. Option B is the answer to a different question, the one that also demands two stationary points.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["root"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "C2-13", "paper": "C2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>For a positive integer $n$, consider the four statements \\[ (1)\\ 4\\mid n,\\qquad (2)\\ 6\\mid n,\\qquad (3)\\ 12\\mid n,\\qquad (4)\\ 8\\mid n . \\] Let $T$ be the set of positive integers satisfying all four. A statement is called <em>removable<\/em> if deleting it from the list does not change the set of integers satisfying all the remaining statements. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["Each of the four statements is removable.", "Only statement (4) is not removable.", "Only statement (1) is not removable.", "Only statements (3) and (4) are not removable.", "No statement is removable.", "Only statements (2) and (4) are not removable."], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>All four together mean $8\\mid n$ and $3\\mid n$, that is $24\\mid n$. Remove (1): $6,12,8\\mid n$ still gives $24\\mid n$. Remove (2): $4,12,8$ gives $24$. Remove (3): $\\operatorname{lcm}(4,6,8)=24$. Remove (4): $\\operatorname{lcm}(4,6,12)=12$, so $n=12$ now slips in. Only (4) is not removable. Note that removability is about one statement at a time: (1), (2) and (3) are each removable, but not all three together.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "mod"], "rating": 6.8}, {"id": "B2-8", "paper": "B2", "n": 8, "q": "<p>What is the smallest positive integer $n$ with the following property: among any $n$ integers there are two whose sum or whose difference is divisible by $12$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$6$", "$7$", "$8$", "$12$", "$13$", "$24$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Two integers have sum or difference divisible by $12$ exactly when their remainders on division by $12$ are equal or add to $12$. Group the remainders into the classes $\\{0\\}$, $\\{6\\}$, $\\{1,11\\}$, $\\{2,10\\}$, $\\{3,9\\}$, $\\{4,8\\}$, $\\{5,7\\}$: seven classes, and two numbers from the same class always work. So $8$ integers force a pair. Seven can avoid it: the remainders $0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6$ are all distinct and no two of them sum to $12$. The answer is $8$. Option B is the count of classes, one short of the pigeonhole answer.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["pig", "mod"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "C1-15", "paper": "C1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Let $f(x)=\\tfrac12\\left(x^3+x\\right)$. Find the area of the finite region in the first quadrant bounded by the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=f^{-1}(x)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{1}{16}$", "$\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac16$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac13$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>$f$ is increasing and odd, and $f(x)=x$ exactly when $x^3=x$, so the graphs of $f$ and $f^{-1}$ meet the line $y=x$, and each other, at $0$ and $1$ in the first quadrant. On $(0,1)$, $f(x)&lt;x$. The region between $f$ and $f^{-1}$ is symmetric about $y=x$, so its area is twice the area between $y=x$ and $y=f(x)$: \\[ 2\\int_0^1\\left(x-\\tfrac12(x^3+x)\\right)dx=\\int_0^1(x-x^3)\\,dx=\\tfrac14 . \\] No inverse function needs to be found. Option B is the area between $f$ and the line $y=x$ alone.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "sym"], "rating": 6.9}, {"id": "B1-18", "paper": "B1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The points $A(1,2)$ and $B(5,2)$ are fixed, and $C$ is a point such that $\\angle ACB = 45^\\circ$. Find the maximum possible area of triangle $ABC$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$2 + 2\\sqrt2$", "$4\\sqrt2$", "$4 + 2\\sqrt2$", "$4 + 4\\sqrt2$", "$8$", "$8\\sqrt2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>The angle at $C$ is fixed, so $C$ lies on an arc of a circle through $A$ and $B$, and the sine rule in the circumcircle gives $2R = \\dfrac{AB}{\\sin 45^\\circ} = \\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt2/2}$, so $R = 2\\sqrt2$. The centre is at distance $\\sqrt{R^2 - 2^2} = 2$ from $AB$. Since $45^\\circ$ is acute, $C$ is on the major arc, on the far side of the centre from $AB$, and the largest height is $2 + 2\\sqrt2$ at the midpoint of that arc. The maximum area is $\\tfrac12\\cdot 4\\cdot(2+2\\sqrt2) = 4 + 4\\sqrt2$. Option A is the height, not the area.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "B2-15", "paper": "B2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Each cell of a $6 \\times 6$ grid is coloured black or white. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Every row contains more black cells than white cells, and every column contains more white cells than black cells.<\/li><li>Every row contains an odd number of black cells, and every column contains an odd number of black cells.<\/li><li>Every $2 \\times 2$ block of four adjacent cells contains exactly one black cell.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements can be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I is impossible by counting black cells two ways: each row has at least $4$ black cells, giving at least $24$ in total, while each column has at most $2$, giving at most $12$. II is possible: colour the six cells on a diagonal black, so every row and column has exactly one. III is possible: colour black exactly the cells whose row number and column number are both even; any two consecutive rows contain exactly one even row and any two consecutive columns exactly one even column, so every $2 \\times 2$ block contains exactly one black cell. The answer is II and III. Note that for II the parity check passes (six odd rows and six odd columns both give an even total) but passing a parity check is not a construction; the diagonal is.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["col", "two"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "C1-11", "paper": "C1", "n": 11, "q": "<p>The function $F$ is defined for all real $x$ by \\[ F(x)=\\int_0^x (x-t)\\sin t\\,dt . \\] Find $F(\\pi)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$0$", "$1$", "$\\pi-2$", "$2$", "$3$", "$\\pi$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>The variable $x$ appears inside the integrand, so take it outside first: \\[ F(x)=x\\int_0^x\\sin t\\,dt-\\int_0^x t\\sin t\\,dt=x(1-\\cos x)-(\\sin x-x\\cos x)=x-\\sin x . \\] So $F(\\pi)=\\pi$. The integration by parts is routine; the step that matters is noticing that $x$ is a constant as far as the $t$-integration is concerned and pulling it out.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "sym"], "rating": 7.0}, {"id": "B1-5", "paper": "B1", "n": 5, "q": "<p>Positive real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy $x + y = 1$. Find the minimum value of \\[ \\Bigl(x + \\frac1x\\Bigr)^2 + \\Bigl(y + \\frac1y\\Bigr)^2 . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$8$", "$\\dfrac{25}{4}$", "$\\dfrac{25}{2}$", "$13$", "$15$", "$25$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Put $u = x + \\frac1x$ and $v = y + \\frac1y$. Then $u + v = 1 + \\frac1x + \\frac1y \\ge 1 + \\frac{4}{x+y} = 5$, the inequality being $\\frac1x + \\frac1y \\ge \\frac{4}{x+y}$ (AM--HM, or clear denominators to $(x-y)^2 \\ge 0$). Then \\[ u^2 + v^2 \\ge \\frac{(u+v)^2}{2} \\ge \\frac{25}{2}, \\] with equality throughout at $x = y = \\tfrac12$, where each bracket is $\\tfrac52$. Substituting $y = 1 - x$ and differentiating is possible but ugly, and the two-step inequality is what the question is testing.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ineq"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "C1-13", "paper": "C1", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Evaluate \\[ \\int_0^4 \\frac{1}{1+2^{\\,x-2}}\\,dx . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{2}{\\ln 2}$", "$4-\\dfrac{2}{\\ln 2}$", "$\\ln 5$", "$\\dfrac52$"], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Let $g(x)=\\frac{1}{1+2^{x-2}}$ and pair $x$ with $4-x$. Writing $t=2^{x-2}$, we have $2^{(4-x)-2}=\\frac1t$, so \\[ g(x)+g(4-x)=\\frac{1}{1+t}+\\frac{1}{1+\\frac1t}=\\frac{1}{1+t}+\\frac{t}{1+t}=1 . \\] Substituting $x\\mapsto4-x$ shows $\\int_0^4g(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^4g(4-x)\\,dx$, so twice the integral is $\\int_0^4 1\\,dx=4$, and the answer is $2$. Integrating directly is possible, via $\\frac{1}{1+t}=1-\\frac{t}{1+t}$, but slower and error-prone.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "arint"], "rating": 7.1}, {"id": "C1-7", "paper": "C1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>For how many integers $k$ does the equation \\[ 2\\sin^2x+3\\sin x+k=0 \\] have exactly two solutions with $0\\le x&lt;2\\pi$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$1$", "$2$", "$3$", "$4$", "$5$", "$6$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Put $s=\\sin x\\in[-1,1]$. A value of $s$ strictly between $-1$ and $1$ gives two values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi)$; $s=\\pm1$ gives one each. Write the equation as $k=g(s)=-2s^2-3s$. On $[-1,1]$, $g$ rises from $g(-1)=1$ to its maximum $g(-\\frac34)=\\frac98$, then falls to $g(1)=-5$. For an integer $k$ with $-5&lt;k&lt;1$, the horizontal line meets the falling branch exactly once, at some $s$ strictly inside $(-1,1)$, and misses the rising branch: exactly two solutions. At $k=1$ the roots are $s=-1$ and $s=-\\frac12$, giving $1+2=3$ solutions; at $k=-5$ the only admissible root is $s=1$, giving $1$. So $k\\in\\{-4,-3,-2,-1,0\\}$: five integers.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "root"], "rating": 7.2}, {"id": "B1-7", "paper": "B1", "n": 7, "q": "<p>Find the value of \\[ \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{n+2}{n(n+1)\\,2^{n}} . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac34$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac54$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Partial fractions in $n$ alone go nowhere, because of the $2^n$. The trick is to split the numerator against the factor $2$ rather than against $n$ and $n+1$: write $n + 2 = 2(n+1) - n$, so \\[ \\frac{n+2}{n(n+1)2^n} = \\frac{2}{n\\,2^n} - \\frac{1}{(n+1)2^n} = \\frac{1}{n\\,2^{n-1}} - \\frac{1}{(n+1)\\,2^{n}} . \\] With $b_n = \\dfrac{1}{n\\,2^{n-1}}$ this is $b_n - b_{n+1}$, so the partial sum is $b_1 - b_{N+1} = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{(N+1)2^N}$, and the infinite sum is $1$. The first three partial sums are $\\tfrac34, \\tfrac{11}{12}, \\tfrac{31}{32}$, which is the pattern a candidate without the decomposition would have to guess from; option B is the first term on its own.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["tel", "ser"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "C2-15", "paper": "C2", "n": 15, "q": "<p>For a real number $x$, consider the five statements \\[ x>1,\\qquad x^2>1,\\qquad x^3>1,\\qquad x>-1,\\qquad x^2>x . \\] Find the complete set of real $x$ for which exactly two of these statements are true.<\/p>", "opts": ["$x&lt;-1$", "$-1&lt;x&lt;0$", "$x&lt;0$", "$x\\le 0$ and $x\\ne-1$", "$x&lt;0$ and $x\\ne-1$", "there is no such $x$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Count true statements region by region. For $x>1$ all five hold. For $-1&lt;x\\le1$, $x>-1$ holds, and $x^2>x$ holds only when $x&lt;0$; so two are true on $(-1,0)$ and one on $[0,1]$. At $x=-1$ only $x^2>x$ holds. For $x&lt;-1$, exactly $x^2>1$ and $x^2>x$ hold. So exactly two are true for $x&lt;-1$ and for $-1&lt;x&lt;0$, but not at $x=-1$ itself.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["log", "root"], "rating": 7.3}, {"id": "B2-13", "paper": "B2", "n": 13, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a continuous function on $[0,1]$ with $0 \\le f(x) \\le 1$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)^2\\,dx \\le \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)^2\\,dx \\le \\left(\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx\\right)^{\\!2}$<\/li><li>If $\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)^2\\,dx = \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$, then $f$ is constant on $[0,1]$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>I holds because $0 \\le f \\le 1$ gives $f^2 \\le f$ pointwise, and integrating preserves the inequality. II is false: $f(x) = x$ gives $\\int f^2 = \\tfrac13$ and $(\\int f)^2 = \\tfrac14$. The true inequality runs the other way, and the neatest proof is worth knowing: with $m = \\int_0^1 f$, expanding $0 \\le \\int_0^1 (f - m)^2 = \\int f^2 - 2m^2 + m^2$ gives $\\int f^2 \\ge m^2$. For III, the hypothesis says $\\int_0^1 (f - f^2) = 0$ with $f - f^2 = f(1-f) \\ge 0$ continuous, so $f(1 - f)$ vanishes everywhere: every value of $f$ is $0$ or $1$. A continuous function taking only the values $0$ and $1$ cannot take both (it would have to pass through $\\tfrac12$), so $f$ is constant. The answer is I and III. Option D is the reader who assumes an inequality between $\\int f^2$ and $(\\int f)^2$ without checking which way it goes.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "ineq"], "rating": 7.4}, {"id": "B1-1", "paper": "B1", "n": 1, "q": "<p>Find the exact value of $\\cos\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}\\cos\\dfrac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\dfrac{3\\pi}{7}$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac1{16}$", "$\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac14$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt7}{8}$", "$-\\dfrac18$", "$\\dfrac12$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Let $P = \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{7}\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{7}$ and multiply by $\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{7}$. Folding three times with $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = \\sin 2\\theta$, \\[ \\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{7}\\,P = \\tfrac12\\sin\\tfrac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\tfrac{2\\pi}{7}\\cos\\tfrac{4\\pi}{7} = \\tfrac14\\sin\\tfrac{4\\pi}{7}\\cos\\tfrac{4\\pi}{7} = \\tfrac18\\sin\\tfrac{8\\pi}{7} = -\\tfrac18\\sin\\tfrac{\\pi}{7}, \\] so $P = -\\tfrac18$. Since $\\cos\\frac{3\\pi}{7} = -\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{7}$, the required product is $\\tfrac18$. Option E is $P$ itself, the answer to the question that was not asked; the sign flip on the last cosine is the whole point.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "sym"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "C1-20", "paper": "C1", "n": 20, "q": "<p>Find \\[ \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\ \\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\frac{n}{(n+k)^2}\\, . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$2\\ln 2-1$", "$\\dfrac12$", "$\\ln 2$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$", "$1$", "the limit does not exist"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Factor out $\\frac1n$: the sum is $\\frac1n\\sum_{k=1}^n\\frac{1}{(1+k/n)^2}$, a Riemann sum for $\\int_0^1\\frac{dx}{(1+x)^2}$. As $n\\to\\infty$ it tends to \\[ \\left[-\\frac{1}{1+x}\\right]_0^1=1-\\frac12=\\frac12 . \\] The recognition is the question: once the sum is seen as rectangles of width $\\frac1n$ under a curve, the limit is an area.<\/p>", "tags": ["arint", "ser"], "rating": 7.5}, {"id": "B1-15", "paper": "B1", "n": 15, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $k$ for which the equation \\[ 4^x + 4^{-x} = k\\left(2^x + 2^{-x}\\right) - 4 \\] has exactly two real solutions in $x$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$k \\ge 2\\sqrt2$", "$k > 2\\sqrt2$", "$k \\ge 3$", "$k > 3$", "$|k| > 3$", "$k > 2$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Let $t = 2^x + 2^{-x}$, so $t \\ge 2$ by AM--GM, with $t = 2$ only at $x = 0$, and $4^x + 4^{-x} = t^2 - 2$. The equation becomes $t^2 - kt + 2 = 0$. Each root $t > 2$ gives two values of $x$ (symmetric under $x \\mapsto -x$), $t = 2$ gives one, and $t &lt; 2$ gives none. The roots multiply to $2$, so at most one of them can be $\\ge 2$. Hence exactly two solutions in $x$ needs exactly one root $t > 2$, i.e.\\ the quadratic $h(t) = t^2 - kt + 2$ satisfies $h(2) &lt; 0$, which is $6 - 2k &lt; 0$, so $k > 3$. At $k = 3$ the roots are $2$ and $1$, giving only $x = 0$. Option B is the discriminant condition and ignores the constraint $t \\ge 2$ entirely.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["sym", "root"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "C1-8", "paper": "C1", "n": 8, "q": "<p>Find the total length of the set of values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi]$ for which \\[ \\sin x+\\sin 2x+\\sin 3x>0 . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$", "$\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>Pair the outer terms: $\\sin x+\\sin3x=2\\sin2x\\cos x$, so the expression is $\\sin2x\\,(2\\cos x+1)$. On $[0,2\\pi]$, $\\sin2x$ changes sign at $\\frac\\pi2,\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}2$, and $2\\cos x+1$ changes sign at $\\frac{2\\pi}3$ and $\\frac{4\\pi}3$. Checking signs on the six intervals, the product is positive on $(0,\\frac\\pi2)$, $(\\frac{2\\pi}3,\\pi)$ and $(\\frac{4\\pi}3,\\frac{3\\pi}2)$, of total length $\\frac\\pi2+\\frac\\pi3+\\frac\\pi6=\\pi$. If the sum-to-product step is unfamiliar, expand $\\sin3x=3\\sin x-4\\sin^3x$ and $\\sin 2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$ to reach the same factorisation.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "C2-16", "paper": "C2", "n": 16, "q": "<p>Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be real numbers and $f(x)=ax^2+bx+c$. Consider the conditions \\[ (1)\\ a>0,\\qquad (2)\\ b^2&lt;4ac,\\qquad (3)\\ c>0,\\qquad (4)\\ f(x)>0\\text{ for every real } x . \\] Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>Conditions (1) and (2) together imply (3).<\/li><li>Condition (4) implies both (1) and (2).<\/li><li>Conditions (2) and (3) together imply (1).<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "D", "sol": "<p>I: $4ac>b^2\\ge0$ and $a>0$ force $c>0$. II fails: $a=b=0$, $c=1$ gives $f\\equiv1>0$, yet (1) fails. The question does not say $f$ is quadratic, and that is the whole trap. III: $4ac>b^2\\ge0$ with $c>0$ forces $a>0$. So I and III.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ns", "poly"], "rating": 7.6}, {"id": "C1-17", "paper": "C1", "n": 17, "q": "<p>Find the total length of the set of values of $x$ in $[0,2\\pi]$ for which \\[ |\\sin x|+|\\cos x|>\\sqrt{\\tfrac32}\\, . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$", "$\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$", "$\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$", "$\\pi$", "$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$", "$\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>Both sides are positive, so squaring is reversible: $(|\\sin x|+|\\cos x|)^2=1+2|\\sin x\\cos x|=1+|\\sin2x|$. The inequality becomes $1+|\\sin2x|>\\frac32$, that is $|\\sin2x|>\\frac12$. As $x$ runs over $[0,2\\pi]$, $2x$ runs over $[0,4\\pi]$, two full periods, and in each period $|\\sin\\phi|>\\frac12$ on a set of length $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$. So the set of $2x$ has length $\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$ and the set of $x$ has length $\\frac{4\\pi}{3}$. Forgetting to halve at the end gives $\\frac{8\\pi}{3}$; forgetting that there are two periods gives option C.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg"], "rating": 7.7}, {"id": "B2-6", "paper": "B2", "n": 6, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a continuous function on $[0,2]$ with $\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = 0$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>There is some $c$ with $0 &lt; c &lt; 2$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_0^c f(x)\\,dx = 0$.<\/li><li>There is some $c$ with $0 &lt; c &lt; 2$ and $f(c) = 0$.<\/li><li>There is some $c$ with $0 \\le c \\le 1$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_c^{c+1} f(x)\\,dx = 0$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Let $F(c) = \\int_0^c f$, so $F(0) = F(2) = 0$. For II, $F$ is differentiable with $F' = f$, and a function that starts and ends at $0$ must have zero derivative somewhere in between (if $F$ is not identically zero it has an interior maximum or minimum, where $F' = 0$; if it is identically zero then $f \\equiv 0$). So II holds. For III, let $g(c) = \\int_c^{c+1} f$ for $0 \\le c \\le 1$. Then $g(0) + g(1) = \\int_0^1 f + \\int_1^2 f = 0$, so $g(0)$ and $g(1)$ are of opposite sign or both zero, and since $g$ is continuous it vanishes somewhere on $[0,1]$. So III holds. I fails: $f(x) = 1 - x$ has $\\int_0^2 f = 0$ but $F(c) = c - \\tfrac12 c^2 = \\tfrac12 c(2-c) > 0$ for $0 &lt; c &lt; 2$. The answer is II and III. The idea in III, adding the two half-length integrals to force a sign change, is worth remembering; it is the reason there is always a unit-length interval on which $f$ has zero average.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint"], "rating": 7.8}, {"id": "C2-20", "paper": "C2", "n": 20, "q": "<p>For each positive integer $n$, $S(n)$ is a statement. It is known that $S(10)$ is true, and that for every positive integer $n$, \\[ \\text{if } S(n) \\text{ is true then } S(n+3) \\text{ is true,}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\text{if } S(n+2) \\text{ is true then } S(n) \\text{ is true.} \\] Which of the following is the set of positive integers $n$ for which $S(n)$ must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\{10\\}$", "$\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}$", "$\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}\\cup\\{13,16,19,\\dots\\}$", "$\\{1,2,\\dots,10\\}$", "$\\{10,11,12,\\dots\\}$", "all positive integers"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>From $S(10)$, the second rule gives $S(8),S(6),S(4),S(2)$. Now combine the rules: if $S(n)$ holds then $S(n+3)$ holds, and then the second rule gives $S(n+1)$. So each true statement makes the next one true, and once $S(1)$ is reached everything follows. It is: $S(2)\\Rightarrow S(5)$, then $S(5)\\Rightarrow S(3)\\Rightarrow S(1)$. So $S(n)$ is true for every positive integer $n$. Seeing that $+3$ followed by $-2$ is a step of $+1$ is the idea; tracking individual implications by hand, you tend to stop at one of the partial sets in the other options.<\/p>", "tags": ["log"], "rating": 7.9}, {"id": "B2-10", "paper": "B2", "n": 10, "q": "<p>$A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ are four points in the plane, no three of which are collinear, with $AB = CD$ and $AD = BC$. Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$ABCD$ is a parallelogram.<\/li><li>The segments $AC$ and $BD$ bisect each other.<\/li><li>Triangles $ABC$ and $CDA$ are congruent.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Triangles $ABC$ and $CDA$ share the side $AC$ and have $AB = CD$, $BC = DA$, so they are congruent by SSS: III must hold. But congruence leaves two positions for $D$ relative to the fixed triangle $ABC$: the reflection of $B$ through the midpoint of $AC$, which makes $ABCD$ a parallelogram, and the reflection of $B$ in the perpendicular bisector of $AC$, which puts $D$ on the same side of $AC$ as $B$. In the second case $ABCD$ is a crossed quadrilateral (its &ldquo;sides&rdquo; $AB$ and $CD$ cross), $BD$ is parallel to $AC$, and neither I nor II holds. Only III must be true. The trap is that &ldquo;two pairs of equal opposite sides&rdquo; is a parallelogram criterion for convex quadrilaterals, and the question never said convex.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ", "log"], "rating": 8.0}, {"id": "B1-3", "paper": "B1", "n": 3, "q": "<p>A polynomial $p$ of degree $4$ satisfies $p(k) = 2^k$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4$. Find $p(5)$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$16$", "$30$", "$31$", "$32$", "$33$", "$64$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>The binomial coefficient $\\binom{x}{j} = \\frac{x(x-1)\\cdots(x-j+1)}{j!}$ is a polynomial in $x$ of degree $j$, and for a non-negative integer $k$ we have $2^k = \\sum_{j=0}^{k}\\binom{k}{j}$, where the terms with $j > k$ vanish. So $p(x) = \\sum_{j=0}^{4}\\binom{x}{j}$ is a quartic with $p(k) = 2^k$ for $k = 0,\\dots,4$, and it is the only one, two quartics agreeing at five points being equal. Then \\[ p(5) = \\sum_{j=0}^{4}\\binom{5}{j} = 2^5 - \\binom55 = 31. \\] The same answer comes from a difference table: the fourth differences of $1,2,4,8,16$ are constant, and extending the table one step gives $31$. The trap is $32$, which assumes a polynomial can imitate $2^x$ one step further than its degree allows.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["poly"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "B2-17", "paper": "B2", "n": 17, "q": "<p>A finite set $S$ of points in the plane has the property that every point of $S$ is the midpoint of the segment joining some two other points of $S$. Which of the following is true?<\/p>", "opts": ["$S$ must be empty.", "$S$ can be non-empty, and any such $S$ has at least $3$ points.", "$S$ can be non-empty, and any such $S$ has at least $4$ points.", "The four vertices of a parallelogram form such a set $S$.", "There is such a set $S$ with exactly $5$ points."], "ans": "A", "sol": "<p>Suppose $S$ is non-empty. Fix any point $O$ and, since $S$ is finite, choose $P \\in S$ furthest from $O$. If $P$ were the midpoint of $XY$ with $X, Y \\in S$ distinct, then $\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\tfrac12\\bigl(\\overrightarrow{OX} + \\overrightarrow{OY}\\bigr)$, and by the triangle inequality \\[ OP \\le \\tfrac12(OX + OY) \\le \\max(OX, OY), \\] with equality throughout only when $\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OY}$, i.e.\\ $X = Y$. Since $X \\ne Y$, one of $OX$, $OY$ is larger than $OP$, contradicting the choice of $P$. So no non-empty finite set has the property, and $S$ must be empty; the condition holds for the empty set vacuously. Options D and E fail for the same reason as everything else: in any finite set, the point furthest out is never a midpoint. Choosing an extreme element and showing it cannot satisfy the condition is the entire method.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["ext"], "rating": 8.2}, {"id": "B1-4", "paper": "B1", "n": 4, "q": "<p>Let $a$, $b$ and $c$ be the three real roots of $x^3 - 3x + 1 = 0$. Find the value of \\[ (a^2-2)(b^2-2)(c^2-2). \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$-3$", "$-1$", "$0$", "$1$", "$3$", "$9$"], "ans": "B", "sol": "<p>Write $f(x) = x^3 - 3x + 1 = (x-a)(x-b)(x-c)$. Then $a^2 - 2 = (a-\\sqrt2)(a+\\sqrt2)$, and \\[ \\prod (a-\\sqrt2) = -f(\\sqrt2), \\qquad \\prod (a+\\sqrt2) = -f(-\\sqrt2), \\] so the required product is $f(\\sqrt2)f(-\\sqrt2) = (1-\\sqrt2)(1+\\sqrt2) = -1$. No root is ever found.<\/p><p>The trigonometric route explains the answer. Putting $x = 2\\cos\\theta$ turns $x^3 - 3x$ into $2\\cos 3\\theta$, so the roots are $2\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$, $2\\cos\\frac{4\\pi}{9}$, $2\\cos\\frac{8\\pi}{9}$, and the map $x \\mapsto x^2 - 2$ is $2\\cos\\theta \\mapsto 2\\cos 2\\theta$, which permutes the three roots. The product is therefore $abc = -1$ by Vieta.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["vie", "poly"], "rating": 8.3}, {"id": "C2-18", "paper": "C2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>The function $f$ is continuous and increasing on $[0,1]$. Which of the following must be true? <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx\\le f(1)$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x f(x)\\,dx\\ge \\frac12\\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx$<\/li><li>$\\displaystyle\\int_0^{1/2} f(x)\\,dx\\le\\int_{1/2}^{1} f(x)\\,dx$<\/li><\/ol><\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "III only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "F", "sol": "<p>I: $f(x)\\le f(1)$ throughout, so the integral is at most $f(1)$. III: substituting $x\\mapsto x+\\frac12$ gives $\\int_{1/2}^1f=\\int_0^{1/2}f(x+\\frac12)\\,dx\\ge\\int_0^{1/2}f(x)\\,dx$. II is the one needing an idea. Since $\\int_0^1(x-\\frac12)\\,dx=0$, \\[ \\int_0^1\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^1\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)\\left(f(x)-f\\left(\\tfrac12\\right)\\right)dx\\ge0, \\] because both brackets are negative for $x&lt;\\frac12$ and non-negative for $x>\\frac12$. That rearranges to II. All three.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint", "ineq"], "rating": 8.4}, {"id": "B1-9", "paper": "B1", "n": 9, "q": "<p>A regular heptagon has side length $1$. Its diagonals take exactly two distinct lengths, $p &lt; q$. Find the value of $\\dfrac1p + \\dfrac1q$.<\/p>", "opts": ["$\\dfrac12$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}{2}$", "$1$", "$\\dfrac{\\sqrt7}{2}$", "$\\dfrac32$", "$2$"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>Label the heptagon $ABCDEFG$, with side $1$, short diagonal $p$ (two steps apart, e.g.\\ $AC$) and long diagonal $q$ (three steps apart, e.g.\\ $AD$). Every regular polygon is cyclic, so Ptolemy applies to any four vertices. On the quadrilateral $ABCE$ the sides are $AB = BC = 1$, $CE = p$, $EA = q$ and the diagonals are $AC = p$, $BE = q$, so Ptolemy gives \\[ AC\\cdot BE = AB\\cdot CE + BC\\cdot EA, \\qquad pq = p + q, \\] so $\\frac1p + \\frac1q = 1$ on dividing by $pq$. The lengths themselves are $p = 2\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{7}$ and $q = 1 + p - \\frac1p$, neither of which is needed. Ptolemy on $ABCD$ gives the companion identity $p^2 = 1 + q$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["circ"], "rating": 8.6}, {"id": "C1-18", "paper": "C1", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Find the complete set of real values of $a$ for which \\[ a\\sin x+\\cos 2x\\le 1+a\\qquad\\text{for all real } x . \\]<\/p>", "opts": ["$0\\le a\\le 4$", "$0\\le a\\le 8$", "$-1\\le a\\le 8$", "$a\\ge -1$", "$a\\ge 0$", "all real $a$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>With $s=\\sin x$ and $\\cos2x=1-2s^2$, the condition is $g(s)=2s^2-as+a\\ge0$ for all $s\\in[-1,1]$. The minimum of $g$ on $[-1,1]$ depends on where the vertex $s=\\frac a4$ lies. If $-4\\le a\\le4$, the minimum is $g(\\frac a4)=a-\\frac{a^2}{8}$, which is non-negative for $0\\le a\\le 8$, so here $0\\le a\\le4$. If $a>4$, the vertex is to the right of the interval and the minimum is $g(1)=2>0$, so every $a>4$ works. If $a&lt;-4$, the minimum is $g(-1)=2+2a&lt;0$. Altogether $a\\ge0$. Option B is the discriminant condition applied without restricting $s$ to $[-1,1]$: it wrongly throws away every $a>8$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["trg", "ineq"], "rating": 8.7}, {"id": "B2-19", "paper": "B2", "n": 19, "q": "<p>The positive integer $n$ is such that $3n + 1$ and $4n + 1$ are both perfect squares. What is the largest integer that must divide $n$?<\/p>", "opts": ["$4$", "$8$", "$14$", "$28$", "$56$", "$112$"], "ans": "E", "sol": "<p>Write $3n + 1 = a^2$ and $4n + 1 = b^2$. Since $4n + 1$ is odd, $b$ is odd, so $b^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$ and $4n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 8$: $n$ is even. Then $3n + 1$ is odd, so $a$ is odd and $a^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 8$, giving $3n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 8$, so $8 \\mid n$. Now modulo $7$: squares are $0, 1, 2, 4$, and $a^2 + b^2 = 7n + 2 \\equiv 2$. The only way two squares sum to $2$ modulo $7$ is $1 + 1$, so $a^2 \\equiv 1$, hence $3n \\equiv 0 \\pmod 7$ and $7 \\mid n$. Therefore $56 \\mid n$. And $n = 56$ itself works: $169 = 13^2$ and $225 = 15^2$, so no larger divisor is forced. The answer is $56$. Option D is what one gets from the mod $7$ argument together with only a partial mod $8$ argument.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["mod"], "rating": 8.8}, {"id": "B2-18", "paper": "B2", "n": 18, "q": "<p>Let $f$ be a continuous function on $[0,1]$ satisfying \\[ \\int_0^1 f(x)\\,dx = 0 \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\int_0^1 x f(x)\\,dx = 0 . \\] Consider: <ol class=\"stmts\" type=\"I\"><li>$f(c) = 0$ for at least one $c$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>$f(c) = 0$ for at least two distinct values of $c$ in $(0,1)$.<\/li><li>$f(x) = 0$ for every $x$ in $[0,1]$.<\/li><\/ol> Which of these statements must be true?<\/p>", "opts": ["I only", "II only", "I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], "ans": "C", "sol": "<p>I: if $f$ were never zero on $(0,1)$ it would keep one sign there by continuity, and its integral could not be $0$. So $f$ changes sign, and has a zero. II: suppose $f$ changes sign at only one point $c$, so $f$ has one sign on $(0,c)$ and the opposite sign on $(c,1)$. Then $(x - c)f(x)$ has the same sign on both intervals and is not identically zero, so $\\int_0^1 (x-c)f(x)\\,dx \\ne 0$. But that integral equals $\\int_0^1 xf - c\\int_0^1 f = 0 - 0$, a contradiction. So $f$ changes sign at least twice, giving at least two zeros; and if $f \\equiv 0$ it has infinitely many. III fails: $f(x) = x^2 - x + \\tfrac16$ has $\\int_0^1 f = \\tfrac13 - \\tfrac12 + \\tfrac16 = 0$ and $\\int_0^1 xf = \\tfrac14 - \\tfrac13 + \\tfrac1{12} = 0$ without vanishing. The answer is I and II. The multiplier $(x - c)$ is the idea: it is a function of one sign built to be orthogonal to nothing but $f$.<\/p><p><\/p>", "tags": ["fint"], "rating": 9.0}];
